.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PATH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_path \- return the URL path of a request
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char *chttpsvr_req_path(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the URL path of the request as a NUL-terminated string,
without the query string.  The string is valid until the handler
call returns.

The path always starts with "/".  A request whose target is neither
an origin-form path nor an absolute-form http or https URL gets
400 Bad Request before any middleware runs.  The one exception is
"OPTIONS *", which the server answers directly with no middleware
and no handler; see chttpsvr_start(3).  For an absolute-form target
the path is the path of that URL, and an empty one becomes "/".

Percent-encoded sequences are decoded by the path rules of RFC 3986,
so the string is the same path that was matched against the route
patterns.  A middleware that runs for a rejected request (see
chttpsvr_use(3)) can see a path with malformed percent-encoding,
which may be the very reason that no route matched.  Such a path has
no decoded form, and the function returns it in its encoded form,
exactly as it arrived.

The decoded path can hold a '/' that arrived as %2F, and "." and
".." segments, which the server neither merges nor removes.  Never
use it as a file name without checking it first.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_raw_query (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_param (3)
